{"name":"Roycroft Bronze Green","hex":"#575449","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bronze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A naturally occurring or man-made alloy of copper, usually in combination with tin, but also with one or more other metals.","A reddish-brown colour, the colour of bronze."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of bronze metal.","Having a reddish-brown colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bronze"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Roycroft","blocks":[{"text":"Roycroft was a reformist community of craft workers and artists which formed part of the Arts and Crafts movement in the United States. Elbert Hubbard founded the community in 1895, in the village of East Aurora, New York, near Buffalo. Participants were known as Roycrofters. The work and philosophy of the group, often referred to as the Roycroft movement, had a strong influence on the development of American architecture and design in the early 20th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Roycroft_Copper_Shop.jpg/330px-Roycroft_Copper_Shop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roycroft"},{"title":"Bronze","blocks":[{"text":"Bronze is an alloy consisting primarily of copper, commonly with about 12–12.5% tin and often with the addition of other metals and sometimes non-metals or metalloids. These additions produce a range of alloys some of which are harder than copper alone or have other useful properties, such as strength, ductility, or machinability."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Bull%27s_head_ornament_for_a_lyre_MET_DP260070.jpg/330px-Bull%27s_head_ornament_for_a_lyre_MET_DP260070.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bronze"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"},{"title":"Bronze (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Bronze is a metallic brown color which resembles the metal alloy bronze. An archaic term for it was aeneous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Bronze_medal_for_bravery_%28Austria-Hungary%29.png/330px-Bronze_medal_for_bravery_%28Austria-Hungary%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bronze_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.